# Quickstart

This page takes you from an empty directory to a running workspace: a Fresh app, a typed oRPC
service, Postgres, Redis, and the Aspire resource graph that runs and observes them. Along the way
you learn which files are yours, which files the CLI owns, and which generators to use before you
write a feature by hand.

{{ comp callout { type: "note", title: "Prerequisites" } }}
Install <strong><a href="https://docs.deno.com">Deno 2.x</a></strong> and the
<strong><a href="https://aspire.dev">Aspire CLI</a></strong>, and start Docker. Aspire orchestrates
the local resources; your application code stays Deno and TypeScript. See
<a href="/explanation/aspire/">why NetScript uses Aspire</a> and how to opt out.
{{ /comp }}

## 1. Install the CLI

<!-- quickstart-walk:start -->

```bash
deno install --global --allow-all --name netscript --minimum-dependency-age=0 jsr:@netscript/cli{{ releaseSpecifier }}
```

The command works in Bash and PowerShell. If your shell can't find `netscript` afterwards, add the
directory that Deno prints to your `PATH`.

{{ comp callout { type: "note", title: "Installing on a release day or a canary" } }}
By default, Deno refuses dependencies published in the last ~24 hours. The <code>--minimum-dependency-age=0</code> flag above overrides that. To replace an existing global executable, add <code>-f</code>:
<code>deno install -g -A -f -n netscript --minimum-dependency-age=0 jsr:@netscript/cli{{ releaseSpecifier }}</code>
{{ /comp }}

Check that the CLI is on your path:

```sh
netscript --help
```

The output lists the public command groups: `agent`, `config`, `contract`, `db`, `deploy`,
`generate`, `init`, `marketplace`, `plugin`, `service`, and the `ui:*` family (`ui:list`, `ui:add`,
`ui:update`, `ui:remove`).

## 2. Scaffold the workspace

```bash
netscript init my-app --db postgres --yes
cd my-app
netscript service add --name users --with-client
```

`--yes` accepts the remaining defaults without prompting, including the `dashboard` Fresh app and
the shared Redis cache. `service add` is a separate step because you run the same command later to
add more services. To set an editor explicitly, add `--editor zed`, `--editor vscode`, or
`--editor none`. `netscript init --help` lists every option.

The CLI prints how many files and directories it created. The totals depend on the database,
service, editor, and agent tooling you chose.

### Know what you own

Every entry in the scaffold carries an ownership tag:

- `[owned]` files hold your product code. Your work goes here.
- `[generated]` files are rewritten by a generator. Don't patch them.
- `[guidance]` files describe the local conventions for people and coding agents.

```tree
my-app/
├── AGENTS.md                              # [guidance] added by `agent init`
├── .agents/skills/                        # [generated] canonical `agent init` skills
├── .mcp.json                              # [generated] agent-host MCP wiring
├── .netscript/                            # [generated] CLI/runtime support
├── apps/dashboard/
│   ├── AGENTS.md                          # [guidance] app-specific agent rules
│   ├── WEB-LAYER.md                       # [guidance] contract → page → island map
│   ├── client.ts                          # [owned] Fresh client entry; imports app CSS
│   ├── lib/                               # [owned] per-service data-layer modules
│   │   └── users.ts                       # [owned] users client + query factories
│   ├── utils.ts                           # [owned] web utilities & definePage helper
│   ├── routes/                            # [owned] Fresh pages and route handlers
│   ├── islands/                           # [owned] interactive Preact boundaries
│   ├── components/ui/                     # [owned] copied UI registry source (~60 components)
│   └── .generated/                        # [generated] Fresh manifest and route types
├── contracts/versions/v1/                 # [owned] oRPC routes and Zod boundaries
├── services/users/src/                    # [owned] contract-bound handler logic (main.ts, routers/)
├── database/postgres/
│   ├── schema/schema.prisma               # [owned] persistence models
│   ├── migrations/                        # [generated history] review and commit
│   └── schema/.generated/                 # [generated] Prisma client + Zod schemas
├── plugins/                               # [CLI-managed] installed capability workspaces
├── tests/                                 # [owned] workspace-level integration test suite
├── aspire/
│   ├── apphost.mts                        # [generated] resource-graph entry point
│   └── .helpers/                          # [generated] services/plugins/infrastructure
├── appsettings.json                       # [owned config] resource declarations
├── netscript.config.ts                    # [owned config] framework paths and wiring
├── deno.json                              # [owned config] workspace, imports, and tasks
├── package.json                           # [owned config] Node compatibility marker
└── node_modules/                          # [generated] local materialised npm dependencies
```

Let the CLI decide where files go. The `contract`, `service`, `db`, `plugin`, and `ui:*` generators
put new files where the rest of the workspace and Aspire expect them.

## 3. Start the resource graph

Nothing runs until Aspire starts. From the workspace root, run:

```bash
cd aspire
aspire restore
aspire start
```

In PowerShell, the first step is `Set-Location .\aspire`; the other commands are the same. The
Windows guidance on this page comes from the CLI source and has not been run on Windows.

{{ comp callout { type: "tip", title: "Use the start wrapper" } }}
The next steps that the CLI prints recommend <code>deno task aspire:start</code> instead of a bare <code>aspire start</code>. It runs the same command with <code>ASPIRE_CLI_START_TIMEOUT=300</code>, which gives container images 300 seconds to start on the first run. Use the wrapper unless you are reproducing this walk exactly.
{{ /comp }}

In an interactive terminal, `aspire start` keeps running: leave it open, then open the dashboard URL
and login token that it prints. In a non-interactive shell, such as a CI job or an agent tool call,
`aspire start` detaches. It prints the AppHost PID and the dashboard URL, then returns while the
resources keep running. The dashboard needs a browser and a login token, so read the running graph
from the shell instead:

```sh
aspire describe --apphost ./aspire/apphost.mts --format Json --non-interactive --nologo
```

It prints every resource with its allocated endpoint: the Fresh app, the service, Postgres, and
Redis. From an agent host, the MCP tool `list_api_services` returns the `baseUrl`, `specUrl`, and
`docsUrl` of each resource. Don't use `get_app_status` or `doctor` to find endpoints:
`get_app_status` returns only a health verdict and aggregate counts, without names or URLs, and
reads a telemetry endpoint that defaults to a port Aspire did not allocate; `doctor` checks project
files on disk, not running processes.

Aspire starts Postgres and Redis, injects service discovery, assigns free ports at runtime, runs
health checks, and collects logs and distributed traces. Because ports are assigned at runtime,
there is no application or service port to memorise. When a copied port, an old terminal, and the
running system disagree, trust the dashboard, `aspire describe`, or `list_api_services`.

{{ comp callout { type: "note", title: "Prefer no orchestration?" } }}
Aspire is the default, not a requirement. The AppHost is generated TypeScript (<code>aspire/apphost.mts</code>) that runs on an isolated Node runtime inside <code>aspire/</code>; you write no C#. To skip it, scaffold with <code>netscript init my-app --db postgres --no-aspire</code> and start the app with <code>deno task --cwd apps/dashboard dev</code>; Postgres, the cache, and every connection string are then yours to provide. <a href="/explanation/aspire/#the---no-aspire-escape-hatch">What you give up</a>.
{{ /comp }}

Wait until the Fresh app, the `users` service, Postgres, and Redis report healthy. Open a resource
in the dashboard to read its console logs. After you make a request, open the trace view to follow
it across processes. [Aspire & the AppHost](/explanation/aspire/) and
[Observability](/observability/) explain the full model.

## 4. Prepare the database

Leave Aspire running; if it holds your terminal, open a second one. The `cd ..` below returns from
`aspire/` to the workspace root. Each `netscript db` command starts an operation resource inside the
running AppHost and uses its existing Postgres connection. It doesn't provision another database or
mount `.data/postgres` again.

```bash
cd ..
netscript db init --db postgres --name init
netscript db generate --db postgres
netscript db seed --db postgres
deno task check
```

<!-- quickstart-walk:end -->

This order is required for an Aspire-managed database. If the AppHost is stopped, a `db` command
fails fast instead of rebuilding the resource graph.

- `db init` creates and applies the first Prisma migration. Without it, the scaffold has no tables.
- `db generate` produces the Prisma client and the Zod model schemas in `crud.ts`.
- `db seed` runs the workspace seed script.

`db init` is the first end-to-end checkpoint. When it succeeds, the AppHost resource, the connection
string, the Prisma schema, and the migration path agree, before any product code can hide a failure.

### How the database contract is derived

After `db generate`, `@database/zod` exports the model schemas and CRUD input schemas. In
`deno.json`, it resolves to `./database/<engine>/schema/.generated/zod/crud.ts`, a NetScript-owned
aggregate that exports `<Model>Schema`, `<Model>CreateInput`, and `<Model>UpdateInput` for every
model in `schema.prisma`. Derive API contracts from it instead of duplicating Prisma models:

```ts
import { UserSchema as DatabaseUserSchema } from '@database/zod';
import { z } from 'zod';

// The scaffolded User model has id, name, createdAt, updatedAt. Pick only fields
// that exist in schema.prisma; introduce new public fields with .extend().
export const UserSchemaV1 = DatabaseUserSchema
  .pick({ id: true, name: true, createdAt: true })
  .extend({
    name: z.string().min(1).max(120),
    status: z.enum(['draft', 'active', 'archived']),
  });
```

The generated schema stays the source for column types and nullability. The contract decides what
crosses the boundary and adds the stricter public rules; [Contracts & type flow](/explanation/contracts/)
explains the split. Re-run `netscript db generate` to rebuild `crud.ts` for all models after you
change `schema.prisma`.

## Verify your progress

Check every item before you customise anything. An unverified base makes every later failure look
like a bug in your code.

- [ ] `netscript --help` lists the public command groups (`agent`, `config`, `contract`, `db`, `deploy`, `generate`, `init`, `marketplace`, `plugin`, `service`, `ui:*`).
- [ ] `my-app/` contains `apps/`, `contracts/`, `services/`, `database/`, `plugins/`, `aspire/`, and `tests/`.
- [ ] Aspire is running, and the dashboard or `aspire describe --apphost ./aspire/apphost.mts --format Json` shows the Fresh app, the `users` service, Postgres, and Redis with endpoints. MCP `list_api_services` covers only the HTTP resources, the Fresh app and the service; Postgres and Redis publish `tcp://` endpoints and don't appear there.
- [ ] `netscript db init --name init` succeeded and the initial migration exists.
- [ ] The Fresh app answers at the URL shown in the dashboard or by `aspire describe`.
- [ ] `<fresh-app-url>/design` renders the generated design reference. It answers with an HTTP 302 redirect to `/design/tokens`, so scripted checks need `curl -L`.
- [ ] `deno task check` passes with no errors.

### Troubleshooting

- Check that Aspire is still running and its resources are healthy.
- Check that Docker is running, with `docker info`.
- Check that you ran `aspire restore` in `aspire/` before `deno task aspire:start`.
- Under heavy system load, `aspire restore` or `aspire start` can time out with
  `Failed to prepare: A task was canceled`. Running the start command again usually succeeds.
- Without a browser, read failure diagnostics with the MCP `get_recent_errors` tool and resolve
  endpoints with `aspire describe --apphost ./aspire/apphost.mts --format Json`. Don't guess the
  process state.
- On Windows, a generated `deno task deps:verify` error names an incomplete project-local npm
  materialisation and prints the PowerShell recovery command. Run that command; don't patch a
  package.

## Tour the generated pages

Open two generated pages before you edit anything:

- **`<fresh-app-url>/design`** renders the tokens, components, and composition rules already in the
  app. Read `/design/composition` before you decide what belongs on the server and what belongs in
  an island.
- **`<service-url>/api/docs`** is the service's Scalar API reference, generated from the contract
  that the handler implements. Use it to inspect and call the running oRPC/OpenAPI surface.

Take both base URLs from the dashboard, `aspire describe`, or `list_api_services`.

## Build a feature through the CLI

Build features with the generators instead of creating folders by hand. From the workspace root,
add an `orders` contract with one route, then a service and its handler stub:

```sh
netscript contract add orders
netscript contract add-route orders recent --method GET --path /orders/recent
netscript service add --name orders --with-client
netscript service add-handler orders recent
```

- `contract add` creates the v1 contract, and `add-route` appends the `recent` procedure.
- `service add --with-client` creates the service workspace, registers it with Aspire, and writes
  the typed data layer at `apps/dashboard/lib/orders.ts`.
- `service add-handler` binds that procedure in the correct router with a stub that compiles and
  throws `Not implemented` at runtime.

Replace the throw with your business logic. Don't hand-write a parallel router.

{{ comp callout { type: "important", title: "Restart Aspire after adding a service" } }}
<code>service add</code> regenerates the Aspire helper files under <code>aspire/.helpers/</code>, but a running AppHost doesn't pick up the new service graph. Stop it with <code>aspire stop --apphost ./aspire/apphost.mts</code> and start it again with <code>deno task aspire:start</code> before you look for the new service in the dashboard or the MCP tools.
{{ /comp }}

When the feature needs a new table, edit `database/postgres/schema/schema.prisma`, then migrate and
regenerate so the database and the generated types stay in step:

```sh
netscript db migrate --name add_orders
netscript db generate
```

### Pages and UI components

The app comes with a component registry under `apps/dashboard/components/ui/`: more than 60
components, including `data-table`, `stats-grid`, `detail-layout`, `empty-state`, `panel`,
`filter-form`, and `chart-block`. The `ui:*` commands list, copy, update, and remove registry items:

```sh
netscript ui:list --project-root apps/dashboard
```

{{ comp callout { type: "warning", title: "Always pass --project-root" } }}
Every <code>ui:*</code> command needs <code>--project-root apps/dashboard</code>. Without it, the commands resolve to the workspace root, where <code>components/ui/</code> doesn't exist: <code>ui:list</code> reports the components the scaffold already copied as missing, and <code>ui:add</code> writes a second copy into the workspace root and edits the root <code>deno.json</code>.
{{ /comp }}

Use an existing registry item before you build UI by hand:

- `netscript ui:add <item> --project-root apps/dashboard` copies an item.
- `ui:update --project-root apps/dashboard` syncs the copies you haven't modified.
- `ui:remove <item> --project-root apps/dashboard` removes an item.

To scaffold a Fresh page with a colocated island and query loader, run:

```sh
netscript ui:add page orders --island --project-root apps/dashboard
```

The generator writes `apps/dashboard/routes/orders/index.tsx`, `OrdersIsland.tsx`, and
`query-loaders.ts`. `deno task check` covers only `.ts` files under `apps/`, `services/`, and
`contracts/`, so type-check the generated `.tsx` files directly:

```sh
deno check --unstable-kv "apps/dashboard/routes/orders/index.tsx" "apps/dashboard/routes/orders/(_islands)/OrdersIsland.tsx"
```

The generated page compiles and has the correct file layout. Connect its query loader to the data
layer in `apps/dashboard/lib/orders.ts`, then implement the feature logic.

For a breaking contract change, start with `netscript contract version --help`. To add a background
capability, use `netscript plugin install <kind>`. For example,
`netscript plugin install worker --name workers` scaffolds the plugin workspace and registers it
with Aspire. The [CLI reference](/cli-reference/) groups the everyday commands by task.

## Set up coding agents

If a coding agent will work in the project, install its local guidance and MCP configuration:

```sh
netscript agent init
```

Add `--host claude|vscode|all`, `--editor none|zed|vscode`, or `--with-docs` as needed. Re-running
the command preserves unrelated host configuration.

`agent init` configures the client to launch `netscript agent mcp`, a standard-input/output server
that the client starts; you don't run it by hand. Reach for `doctor` first when the stack is red,
`find_export` before you guess a package subpath, `list_api_services` to resolve runtime URLs
without a browser, and `get_operation_schema` before you write a request with `curl`.
[Agent tooling](/ai/agent-tooling/) documents every tool, its schema, and its safety policy.

## Stop the workspace

When you finish, stop the AppHost from the workspace root:

```sh
aspire stop --apphost ./aspire/apphost.mts
```

The Postgres container is declared `Persistent` in `appsettings.json`, so it survives AppHost
shutdown. To remove the container and free disk space, use `docker stop` and `docker rm`.

Next: build a complete system, chapter by chapter, in the
[Storefront tutorial](/tutorials/storefront/). For a single task, such as adding a service, a
background job, a screen, or sign-in, pick a recipe in [Build](/how-to/).
